we begin this morning with president  biden who is spending his second day in  saudi arabia for a high-stakes meeting  with the leaders of oil-producing arab  states among the issues they will  discuss global energy security high oil  prices and middle east peace the summit  comes on the heels of the president's  friday interaction with controversial  saudi leader muhammad bin salman ed  o'keefe is traveling with the president  he is in the city of jeddah the red sea  joins us now ed good morning  dana good morning great to be with you  the president so far this morning has  held meetings with the leaders of iraq  egypt and other countries as he prepares  to lay out his broader visions for the  middle east somewhere he's eager to  continue exerting influence even if it  means taking criticism for something  that transpired last night  it was the fist bump seen round the  world giving the media savvy saudi crown  prince mohammed bin salman a viral  moment he long sought president biden  was reluctant to hold the meeting given  that he'd denounced the prince after u.s  intelligence confirmed mbs had ordered  the murder of one of his most prominent  critics washington post contributing  columnist jamal khashoggi on friday the  president said he raised the killing at  the start of his meeting with the prince  where the fist bumping continued with  other top u.s officials  i raised it at the top of the meeting  making it clear what i thought of it at  the time  and what i think of it now and it was  exactly i was straightforward and direct  in discussing it i made my view crystal  clear mr biden was asked how mbs  responded he basically said that he  he was not personally responsible for it  i indicated i thought he was there was a  clear difference of opinion confirmed by  saudi minister of state for foreign  affairs adele al-jabir who was in the  meeting the crown prince himself did not  take blame for the murder the charges  that the crown prince knew about it or  much less ordered it are ridiculous  khashoggi's fiancee tweeted a  photoshopped image of what he might have  thought of the meeting writing the blood  of mbs's next victim is on your hands  i'm sorry she feels that way the post's  publisher denounced the shameful scene  is giving mbs the unwarranted redemption  he's been desperately seeking the  president is expected to leave saudi  arabia today without explicit assurance  as the saudis will boost oil production  to ease gas prices but opec leaders are  set to discuss doing so next month  i suspect you won't see that for another  couple weeks al-jabir said the problem  for the u.s isn't global oil supply but  its own lack of refining capacity high  gasoline prices are really a function of  many many things one is the fact that  america has a shortage of refining  capacity no refinery has been built in  decades and so even if you bring more  oil to the market if it cannot be  refined into gasoline it will not lead  to a drop of prices at the pump  and just a few moments ago the president  was seen again alongside mbs at the  beginning of a regional conference of  arab nations he flies home in a few  hours after four intensive days he  agreed with israel that they're going to  work together to stop iran from  developing a nuclear weapon gave out  more aid to the palestinians but  conceded that the israelis and  palestinians aren't going to be able to  hold peace talks of any real substance  for quite some time here in saudi arabia  he was able to stick it to the crown  prince but he leaves with no assurance  from the saudis that they'll help drive  down the price of gasoline an issue  that's likely to dock him in the months  leading up to the midterm elections  adriana  indeed ed thank you
 

